// Word data the T9 game draws from: English words, length 4-8, built from a
// frequency-ranked word list (so inflected/conjugated forms like "faking" or
// "raking" are included, not just root words) intersected with a broad
// dictionary word list, then filtered against WordNet's lemma list to drop
// proper nouns and non-words both source lists let through.
//
// WordNet marks a synset as an 'instance' (@i, e.g. "Comte" the specific
// philosopher) separately from an ordinary class/type relation (@, e.g.
// "hammer" the tool) — a noun lemma is only accepted here if at least one
// of its senses is NOT instance-only, which correctly excludes "della",
// "comte", "aaron", "madonna" etc. (real words that only WordNet-index
// specific named people/places) while keeping dual-use words like "hammer"
// or "iranian". A handful of legitimate words this swept up as collateral
// (e.g. "isle", "fled") are rescued via the same small hand-added list of
// function words and irregular verb/plural forms WordNet does not index at
// all (else, could, went, were, women, ...).
//
// A word counts as real if it, or its stem after stripping a regular
// -s/-es/-ed/-ing/-er/-est suffix (handling e-drop and doubled consonants),
// is in that combined lemma set.
//
// DICTIONARY: the full list, used to check whether a typed guess is a real
// word at all (hangman mode).
// WORDS_GOOD: the subset that is also 'good' under the current t9-logic.js
// rules, precomputed offline (classifying the whole dictionary against every
// possible digit-merge takes the better part of a second — too slow to redo
// on every page load) rather than at runtime. Whenever the good/bad rules in
// t9-logic.js change, or the dictionary itself is regenerated, this needs
// regenerating to match — see the isGoodWord-based filter used to build it.
// WORDS_GOOD_RANK[i] is WORDS_GOOD[i]'s frequency rank (1 = most common) in
// the source frequency list — lower is more common. Used to gauge a hangman
// puzzle's difficulty from how rare its rarest valid answer is.
